<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Graphing Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01977</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Graphing Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2515f08c-2e11-439a-90a0-b426e4837379</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
        <para id="para-00015" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>Consider the quadratic equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Consider the graph of the equation when answering questions a and b.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">a. Where are the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts located?</para>
            <para id="para-00002"/>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00004">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts are at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
        <exercise id="_167a80b4-40da-4f97-8af6-067a60705252" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            
            <para id="para-00005">b. What is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">2. The vertex is halfway between the intercepts at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00008" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>Here is a graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">
                      <media alt="Graph of the parent quadratic function on the coordinate plane. The x-axis scale is 2 and extends from negative 8 to 8. The y-axis scale is 2 and extends from negative 8 to 12.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_12_01.svg" width="200"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00010">Sketch this graph, then sketch a graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> on the same graph. Briefly explain how you know where to sketch the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00011"/>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00013"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is equivalent to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, so the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts are at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Calculate the vertex by finding the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate in between <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> which is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Substitute <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> into the equation to find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>6.188</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The vertex is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>6.188</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The squared term has a positive coefficient so the graph opens upward. Adding a positive linear term to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> shifts the graph down and to the left.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">
                      <media alt="Graph of the parent quadratic function on the coordinate plane and another function labeled as y equals x squared plus 5 times x. The x-axis scale is 2 and extends from negative 8 to 8. The y-axis scale is 2 and extends from negative 8 to 12.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_12_02.svg" width="200"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
</document>